  serviceName="Chiropractic Search Engine Optimization"
  faqs={[
    {
      question: 'What is SEO for chiropractors?',
      answer:
        'Chiropractic SEO improves the website, content, and local business information that help patients discover and evaluate a practice through unpaid search results. The work can include service pages, technical improvements, Google Business Profile information, and measurement. Its value depends on reaching relevant patients and giving them a useful next step.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Should we use SEO or Google Ads first?',
      answer:
        'That depends on your current visibility, website, appointment capacity, and ability to fund a test. Google Ads can place paid search ads in front of people looking for care; SEO addresses your unpaid search presence. We review the current constraint and recommend a scope that fits your practice.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Do we need a new website for SEO?',
      answer:
        'A website review should establish that. Some practices need targeted changes to existing pages; others need a larger rebuild. If a rebuild is needed, preserve useful content and plan redirects for URLs that change so visitors and search engines can reach the replacement pages.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Does Google AI search require special files or schema?',
      answer:
        'No. Google says its AI search features have no special technical requirements beyond normal search eligibility, and no additional AI text files or special schema are required. Useful content, accessible pages, and accurate practice information remain relevant. Other AI services may use different sources and rules.',
    },
    {
      question: 'How long does chiropractic SEO take?',
      answer:
        'Timing depends on the starting condition of your website, the work required, local competition, and how search engines respond. We use the initial review to set priorities and checkpoints. A fixed ranking deadline is not a reliable promise.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Can you guarantee a ranking or an AI recommendation?',
      answer:
        'No. Search engines and AI services control their results. Our proposal defines the work, responsibilities, and review process. Reporting should distinguish search visibility, website visits, inquiries, and booked appointments wherever reliable tracking is available.',
    },
    {
      question: 'What should we bring to the first conversation?',
      answer:
        'Bring your website, main growth priority, service and location details, current marketing arrangement, and a rough picture of new-patient capacity. Available analytics help make the discussion more specific.',
    },
  ]}
  relatedServices={[
    { href: '/services/google-advertising/', label: 'Google Ads for Chiropractors' },
    { href: '/services/web-design/', label: 'Chiropractic Web Design' },
    { href: '/services/facebook-advertising/', label: 'Chiropractic Facebook Ads' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    An established practice has a history that its website should reflect: the team,
    services, locations, and experience that help a prospective patient decide whether to
    call. The starting point for SEO is your next business priority, whether that is an
    associate with open appointments, a service people rarely discover, or another location.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/schedule/">Discuss your practice's SEO priorities</a></p>

  <h2>Start with the problem you need to solve</h2>
  <p>
    An SEO plan should explain what is being changed and why. Before recommending a scope,
    we review your important pages, local search presence, services, booking experience,
    and available reporting. The plan should fit your market and your office's capacity.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, a practice adding a location needs accurate location information and a
    clear route to that office. A practice receiving website inquiries but booking few
    appointments may need improvements to inquiry handling alongside visibility work.
    Those are different assignments.
  </p>

  <h2>What ChiroCandy's chiropractic SEO services can include</h2>
  <p>
    Your proposal identifies the included work, who approves content, and how
    responsibilities are divided between ChiroCandy and your practice.
  </p>

  <h3>Keyword research and service-page improvements</h3>
  <p>
    We review the terms patients use and the services your practice actually offers.
    Page titles, headings, descriptions, images, and internal links help make each page's
    purpose clear. Service pages should explain who provides the service and how to take
    the next step.
  </p>

  <h3>Local SEO and Google Business Profile</h3>
  <p>
    We review your Google Business Profile, relevant business listings, and location
    pages for accurate practice information. Hours, addresses, services, and contact
    details should help nearby patients reach the right office.
  </p>

  <h3>Useful patient content</h3>
  <p>
    We develop service pages and articles around real patient questions, with input and
    review from your practice. Clear explanations of your services and office experience
    help visitors evaluate their options. Clinical information needs review by the
    appropriate professional at your practice.
  </p>

  <h3>Technical SEO and website usability</h3>
  <p>
    We look for missing pages, crawlability problems, unclear navigation, mobile usability
    issues, and slow or difficult booking experiences. Relevant structured data should
    accurately describe the content visitors can see.
  </p>

  <h3>Relevant links and local references</h3>
  <p>
    Where appropriate to the agreed scope, we pursue relevant directory listings and
    legitimate content or outreach opportunities. Links should give people a useful
    route to your practice, with a clear reason for the connection.
  </p>

  <h3>Conversion improvements and reporting</h3>
  <p>
    We review calls to action, forms, and important page layouts alongside search activity.
    Reporting connects the agreed work to relevant visits, inquiries, and appointment
    outcomes where reliable data is available.
  </p>

  <h2>SEO and AI visibility for chiropractors</h2>
  <p>
    AI-generated search answers give practice owners another reason to keep their
    information useful and accurate. Google says established SEO practices remain relevant
    to its AI search features. It does not require additional AI text files, special
    schema, or a prescribed answer length. Read
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features">Google's guidance for AI search features</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A mention in an AI answer, a citation linking to your website, a visit, and an
    appointment are different outcomes. We recommend reviewing the examples behind
    visibility reports and tracking those outcomes separately. One favorable answer
    does not establish a permanent position.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/blog/geo-for-chiropractors/">AI visibility guide for chiropractors</a>
    explains how to evaluate SEO, GEO, and AI search proposals.
  </p>

  <h2>What should your SEO report help you decide?</h2>
  <p>
    A useful review explains the work completed, what changed, what remains uncertain,
    and the next priority. Measures may include search activity for your services,
    visits to important pages, phone and form inquiries, and bookings that can be traced
    reliably. An inquiry is not the same as a patient who attended an appointment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tell us about changes inside the practice too. Staffing, hours, service availability,
    and front-desk capacity affect how marketing translates into patient access.
  </p>

  <h2>Review relevant practice experience</h2>
  <p>
    ChiroCandy has worked with chiropractic practices since 2015. For an example of our
    work across multiple channels, read the
    <a href="/case-studies/ipswich-spine-clinic-amit-patel/">Ipswich Spine Clinic case study</a>.
    It includes a linked Google Business Profile report alongside separately identified
    advertising results. Read the source, reporting window, and metric definitions when
    comparing a case with your own practice.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose the next step for your practice</h2>
  <p>
    If you are weighing channel priorities, our
    <a href="/blog/google-ads-vs-seo-for-chiropractors/">Google Ads vs. SEO guide</a>
    explains the tradeoffs. If you are comparing providers, use our
    <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-chiropractic-marketing-agency/">chiropractic marketing agency checklist</a>
    to compare scope, responsibilities, reporting, and ownership.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bring your growth priority to a conversation with ChiroCandy. We can discuss what
    is working today, where patients encounter friction, and which changes deserve
    attention first.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/schedule/">Book a conversation about your practice</a></p>
